  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nForecast confidence for the weekend into next week remains quite\nlow, especially when looking at moisture levels and rain chances.\nGuidance does agree the subtropical ridge will get pushed to the\neast of our region over the weekend as a Pacific trough moves\nthrough the northern tier states, but the GEFS is stronger and\nfarther south with the trough compared to the EPS. The models also\ndiffer in the amount of moisture and rain chances during the\nperiod with the GEFS favoring better rain chances from Phoenix and\nareas east of Phoenix over the weekend, while the EPS favors\nbetter chances by Tuesday into the middle of next week. Needless\nto say, these differences do not inspire much confidence in the\navailable NBM/WPC PoPs. \n\nThe latest forecast places little if any PoPs on Saturday and \nSaturday night, which seems fairly reasonable, but then seems to \nbe under doing chances late Sunday. The increased PoPs of 20-40% \nthen seem too high for Monday before gradually lowering during the\nmiddle part of next week. Lately, guidance has had a tendency to \nplace fairly high PoPs during the 4-8 day range, just to back off \nas it gets into the short-term. It would seem everything would \nhave to come together nearly perfectly with the trough passing by \nto our north close enough, but not too close to scour out what \nmoisture we have in the low/mid levels. Additionally, if the \nsubtropical ridge becomes more dominant, then we will have to \nworry about too strong of a subsidence inversion. Given the fairly\nhigh model uncertainty, the forecast is likely to change.\n\nThe temperature forecast is also of lower confidence with a fairly\nwide spread starting Monday. At the moment, NBM forecast\ntemperatures call for readings more in the normal range by early\nnext week, but given the higher model spread we could end up\nwith readings slightly below or even slightly above normal.\nGuidance at least mostly agrees on the 'coolest' temperatures\nfalling on next Tuesday and Wednesday before trending upward\nagain during the latter half of next week.",
